    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season died the way too many Maple Leaf seasons die - under its own weight, with the air leaking out long before the final whistle. <strong>Toronto is finished, buried in 8th in the Atlantic Division with a 32-36-14 record, 78 points and a seven-game losing streak</strong>, and no amount of spring sunshine can sand that edge off. This was a club that never found a steady hand, never found enough 5-on-5 punch, and never stopped making the same expensive mistakes. The number that stings most is the one beside its name: 15th. Fifteenth in the league, staring up from the bottom half of the board instead of banging on the glass where contenders live. There were bright flashes, sure, but not enough of them and not from enough places. Now the questions arrive in a hard, cold stack - coaching, roster surgery, which veterans stay, which ones have played their last game in blue and white. The Leafs did not merely miss; they unraveled. <strong>What matters now is whether management finally treats this like a teardown of bad habits - and not another round of wishful thinking - before the offseason clock starts to hammer.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 22, 2026"
      },
