    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "23-27",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays walked into Yankee Stadium battered, judged, and trailing the division's heavyweights - and walked out with something sturdier than a moral victory. <strong>George Springer's homer and a two-hit shutout turned Thursday night into a 2-0 win and a hard-earned split of the four-game set.</strong> That is how you survive May in this league: with a starter-by-committee feel, a bullpen that didn't blink, and just enough muscle from the middle of the order. Toronto is 23-27, third in the AL East, still staring at a wide 11-game gap, but the tone has changed over the last two nights. Win two straight after losing the first two in the Bronx and suddenly the trip home carries a little less dread. Vladimir Guerrero Jr. reached base three times, which is the kind of steady thunder this club needs every night, while the pitching staff held the Yankees to three hits and made every out look expensive. <strong>Now comes Pittsburgh on Friday night, and the Jays need this road-trip spark to become something more than a tidy footnote.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 22, 2026"
      },
