      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft is done, the calendar has turned, and Washington is now living on hope, projection and a roster still trying to catch up to its own ambitions. That's the spring reality in this league - noisy, unforgiving, and built on the thin hope that young legs can patch old holes. <strong>The most important development is the arrival of No. 7 overall pick Sonny Styles, a meat-and-potatoes addition who came after last year's spending spree left Adam Peters with only two top-100 selections.</strong> That matters because the Commanders sit 3rd in the NFC East, 6 games back, with a 1-game winning streak on paper but no games to play and no margin for fantasy. Styles gives them exactly what the roster has lacked - a hard-nosed presence and another body for a defense that needs proven production, not promises. Around him, the fit is obvious: the front office went looking for blue-collar answers, not headline grabs. That is how April's draft was approached. That is how this rebuild must survive. <strong>Now watch whether Styles becomes an immediate snap-eater and whether this class supplies real depth before camp hardens the depth chart.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 22, 2026"
      },
